# Copy to .env and fill in. `docker compose` reads .env from this directory (devops/).
# The compose file refuses to start rather than defaulting any of the blanks.

# ---- GitHub OAuth App --------------------------------------------------------
# Create at github.com/settings/developers -> New OAuth App:
#   Homepage URL                 http://localhost:5173
#   Authorization callback URL   http://localhost:5173/v1/auth/github/callback
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---- Soma --------------------------------------------------------------------
# HS256 key for session tokens and the sign-in state cookie. At least 32 bytes;
# generate one:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
SOMA_SESSION_SECRET=

# Cookies: 0 sets [vars] cookie_secure = false so sign-in works over plain http
# on localhost. Set to 1 as soon as there is TLS in front.
SOMA_COOKIE_SECURE=0

# ---- Postgres ----------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_USER=soma
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=soma

# The `kalam` role's password. Soma's migration creates the role with LOGIN and no
# password -- the credential is deployment configuration, so the committed migration
# ships no secret. The loader sets it on every `up`.
KALAM_DB_PASSWORD=kalam-dev-secret

# ---- The replay store --------------------------------------------------------
# Locally these point at the `minio` service, a real S3 implementation, so the
# presigned PUT the wave does at every finish is exercised rather than stubbed.
# In a deployment they are R2's. The loader makes the bucket.
R2_ENDPOINT=http://minio:9000
# Where the BROWSER fetches a replay from. Soma signs GET URLs against this and never calls it, so
# it must resolve from the browser rather than from the compose network -- SigV4 signs the host, so
# a URL signed for `minio:9000` cannot be rewritten to something reachable after the fact. Unset in
# a deployment, where R2 is public and both are the same address.
R2_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:9000
R2_BUCKET=tinybrains-replays
R2_ACCESS_KEY=tinybrains
R2_SECRET_KEY=tinybrains-dev-secret

# ---- Optional ----------------------------------------------------------------
# ORION_VERSION=1.7.0                # the server binary the orion image downloads
# ORION_UI_VERSION=1.6.0             # the console image; `latest` tracks the newest build
# OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:5173/v1/auth/github/callback
# APP_URL=http://localhost:5173/
# AXON_MEMORY_BUDGET_BYTES=1073741824
# AXON_IDLE_TTL_S=900

# The engine digest is DERIVED from kalam/plugins/tb-ants/tb-ants.wasm by both a
# replica's entrypoint and the loader, so it is never typed. Per-replica overrides
# are KALAM_1_ENGINE_DIGEST / KALAM_2_ENGINE_DIGEST, below.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- docs/deployment.md: the split
# compose/orion/ holds TWO instance configs: soma.toml.tmpl (Soma + Jodi, cluster
# mode) and kalam.toml.tmpl (one per replica, local SQLite). scripts/check/configs.sh asserts the
# three values the split put in two places.

# Cluster mode's shared Redis. Required: Orion refuses to start with cluster.enabled and no Redis.
# REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379

# Which servers the loader installs into. soma and jodi go to one node of the cluster -- an admin
# mutation advances the shared config epoch and every peer resyncs. kalam goes to EACH replica,
# because each has its own state database and there is no epoch bus between them.
#
# ONLY LIST A REPLICA THE PROFILE HAS STARTED, or the loader waits for a container that is not
# there. With `docker compose --profile fleet up`:
# KALAM_ORION_ADMINS=http://kalam-1:8080/api/v1/admin,http://kalam-2:8080/api/v1/admin

# THE DRAIN -- docs/deployment.md §6.2, and these are not the numbers kalam/docs/design.md wrote down. MEASURED on this
# stack, 8 September 2026: with drain=0 force=1 cron=2700, `docker stop` returned in 2 s and left
# 16 rows `running` -- the cron key did NOT protect the wave, because the cron worker is a
# supervised task and main.rs drains those under the SERVER key. With drain=5 force=2700 cron=2700
# the same wave drained clean in 16 s and left nothing behind. drain is an unconditional sleep and
# buys a routeless replica nothing; force is the real bound.
# KALAM_SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECS=5
# KALAM_SHUTDOWN_FORCE_SECS=2700
# KALAM_CRON_SHUTDOWN_SECS=2700
# KALAM_STOP_GRACE=2760s
# SOMA_SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECS=30
# SOMA_SHUTDOWN_FORCE_SECS=30
# SOMA_CRON_SHUTDOWN_SECS=60
# SOMA_STOP_GRACE=90s

# Pin a replica's engine instead of deriving it from the vendored wasm -- how you rehearse a
# rolling deploy: give kalam-2 the new digest and watch kalam-1 drain its own rows and claim none
# of the new ones. The loader refuses a pin that disagrees with the file.
# KALAM_1_ENGINE_DIGEST=
# KALAM_2_ENGINE_DIGEST=

# jodi/docs/rating-and-seasons.md rule 3: days after a season closes before the next may open. A dev stack that wants to
# reopen immediately sets 0. It must be in the SOMA CONTAINER's environment, not only in .env --
# docker-compose.yml passes it through.
# SEASON_GAP_DAYS=1

# The MODEL STORE -- docs/deployment.md §8.1, S3/R2 signed with SigV4. It replaced the shared `axon-store`
# volume, and not as an optimisation: the admission instance and every replica must read and write
# ONE store, or admission succeeds and every match the version is then paired for fails at the
# residency barrier -- quietly, and a long way from the cause. Across hosts there is no shared
# volume, so this is what makes a fleet possible at all.
#
# Two buckets, one credential: replays and models. The loader creates both.
# AXON_STORE_BUCKET=tinybrains-models
# R2_REGION=us-east-1                # R2 signs against "auto"; MinIO and AWS want a real region

# The plugin trust root -- docs/deployment.md §11. Every Orion here reads it as [plugins.trust] public_keys,
# and with it set no plugin loads without a valid detached Ed25519 signature over its component
# digest. Do not invent one: run
#
#   scripts/setup/trust-keygen.sh       mints the pair, writes the private half to keys/, fills this in
#   scripts/setup/sign-plugins.sh       signs every plugin component with it
#
# A deployment uses its own key from the orchestrator's secret store and only ever sets the public
# half here; the server has no signing path at all.
TB_TRUST_PUBLIC_KEY=

# The admin credential for every Orion here -- docs/deployment.md §11. The admin API installs channels,
# workflows, connectors and plugins and reads connector secrets, so it is the trust root for
# putting anything into a server. Mint one:
#
#   scripts/setup/admin-key.sh
#
# Orion accepts either the key or `sha256:<64 hex>` of it in [admin_auth] api_keys, so a deployment
# can keep the digest in config and the key in its orchestrator's secret store. Plaintext entries
# must be at least 32 characters.
ORION_ADMIN_KEY=
